#: R/timeinterval.b.R
msgid ""
"{count} date value(s) were written with a two-digit year. These are read as "
"2000-2068 for 00-68 and as 1969-1999 for 69-99, so a date such as 22/09/68 "
"becomes 2068 and a 1950s-1960s date is pushed a century forward. If any "
"interval is a century off, rewrite the column with four-digit years."
msgstr ""

#: R/timeinterval.b.R
msgid ""
"{count} date values are in the future. Review date columns for data entry "
"errors or incorrect date formats."
msgstr ""

#: R/datetimeconverter.b.R
msgid ""
"{count} date(s) are in the future. Verify this is intentional (for example "
"planned follow-up dates)."
msgstr ""

#: R/datetimeconverter.b.R
msgid ""
"{count} date(s) fall before 1900, which usually means the format was read "
"wrongly or the source has data-entry errors."
msgstr ""

#: R/timeinterval.b.R
msgid ""
"{count} observations ({pct}%) have missing time intervals. Investigate "
"missing date values as this may affect study conclusions."
msgstr ""

#: R/datetimeconverter.b.R
msgid ""
"{count} of {total} dates ({pct}%) are in the future, and this column has "
"two-digit years: a year such as 55 is read as 2055, not 1955. If these dates "
"cannot be in the future - dates of birth, diagnosis or specimen dates - set "
"Two-digit years to 'Always in the past'."
msgstr ""

#: R/datetimeconverter.b.R
msgid ""
"{count} of {total} parsed date(s) fall outside the plausible range (<1900 or "
">1 year in the future); the first is {example}. Review preview to confirm "
"correct parsing."
msgstr ""

